#{[17592186372026 #edb/ref 1080 "18\\pi " #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372026 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372026 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5c50dc6b-1088-5cb6-baa6-a6a933d4f39e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372027 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186372028 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372027 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162372" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372027 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372027 #edb/ref 1071 "Use Stokes' theorem to evaluate \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\text{curl}\\,F=⟨-y,\\,z,\\,-2x⟩,$ and $C$ is the intersection of the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and the plane $z=2+2x-y.$ The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372027 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "00a16c1c-6465-5edc-becb-61f3932da901" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372027 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and the plane $z=2+2x-y.$ One such surface would be the plane $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=2+2x-y.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2+2x-y)=2 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2+2x-y)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨-y,\\,z,\\,-2x⟩{|}_{z=2+2x-y} \\\\  & =⟨{{-y}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{2+2x-y}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{-2x}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(-y)\\cdot (2)-(2+2x-y)\\cdot (-1)-2x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2y+2+2x-y-2x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2+y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ with radius $1$ centered at the origin. Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(2+y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2+r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}2r+{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[{r}^{2}+\\frac{{r}^{3}}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(1+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={[\\theta -\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372028 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372029 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372028 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372030 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372028 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372031 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372028 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372032 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372028 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372033 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372028 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372028 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372028 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186372032 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372028 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "5674f149-9ce3-5602-89ab-1339d40392d5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372029 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{2}{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372029 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372029 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ff3a9c27-f34b-5594-94af-40a7c58297f3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372030 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{4}{3}\\pi " #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372030 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372030 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b5a89c10-18a7-59cb-8a1b-3a18b2a90938" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372031 #edb/ref 1080 "\\pi " #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372031 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372031 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f2ff01ff-40d6-5616-bd3a-9376f9415536" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372032 #edb/ref 1080 "2\\pi " #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372032 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372032 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "14cd568a-621b-5de0-baca-80df932e47d7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372033 #edb/ref 1080 "0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372033 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372033 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "daa9659f-982a-5882-a68f-e91cd62724ff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401336 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186401337 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401336 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162365" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401336 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401336 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $C$ be the intersection of the surface $z=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}$ and the boundary of the infinite vertical column enclosed by the planes $x=\\pm 1,$ and $y=\\pm 1.$ Given that the curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above and $\\text{curl}\\,F=⟨x,\\,y,\\,-2z⟩,$ the circulation of $F$ along $C$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr=\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401336 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a28026ac-9427-5db7-b40f-31513274adc4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401336 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the surface $z=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}$ and the boundary of our infinite vertical column. One such surface is our surface $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1})=\\frac{-2x}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1})=\\frac{-2y}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨x,\\,y,\\,-2z⟩{|}_{z=1/({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =⟨{{x}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{y}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-x\\cdot \\frac{-2x}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}-y\\cdot \\frac{-2y}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-\\frac{2}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/b2/b215ec1e3330f96e7e606e085721b2dd1f50d113f0151e36ec98e6d0a6f54e73.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}-\\frac{2}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,-\\frac{2}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401337 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401338 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401337 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401339 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401337 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401340 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401337 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401341 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401337 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186401342 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401337 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401337 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401337 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186401341 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401337 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "17de6bba-7478-572d-9fe3-ada91a6e9edc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401338 #edb/ref 1080 "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,-\\frac{4}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401338 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401338 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "69eaa3fe-4c03-532c-9d1a-cd84b8703ad2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401339 #edb/ref 1080 "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,-\\frac{xy}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401339 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401339 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "460112aa-e36b-52a6-a02b-783fee9d05b2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401340 #edb/ref 1080 "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401340 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186401340 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9d2fbcee-821a-5cd9-8a6e-f9412bd4ab5e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1759218640