86045417] [17592186521464 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=x\\,i-z\\,k$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=({t}^{2}-t)\\,i+({t}^{3}-{t}^{2})\\,j+k$ for $t\\in [0,1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521464 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "19360ab0-63c4-5cd8-9a4a-b29910944cd3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521464 #edb/ref 1074 "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}-t,\\,y={t}^{3}-{t}^{2},\\,z=1.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =x\\,i-z\\,k \\\\  & =({t}^{2}-t)\\,i-1\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-{t}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)\\,k \\\\  & =(2t-1)\\,i+(3{t}^{2}-2t)\\,j+0\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(({t}^{2}-t)\\,i-\\,k)\\cdot ((2t-1)\\,i+(3{t}^{2}-2t)\\,j) \\\\  & =({t}^{2}-t)\\cdot (2t-1)+0\\cdot (3{t}^{2}-2t)+(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =({t}^{2}-t)(2t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}-t)(2t-1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\frac{({t}^{2}-t)^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(0-0) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521469 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521421 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521469 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162363" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521469 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521469 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $C$ be the intersection of the hyperbolic paraboloid $z={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and the boundary of the infinite vertical column enclosed by the planes $x=\\pm 1,$ $y=\\pm 2.$ Given that the curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above and $\\text{curl}\\,F=⟨y,\\,x,\\,2⟩,$ the circulation of $F$ along $C$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr=\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521469 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "22c4166d-7812-5cc0-89d7-bc30157982f0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521469 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the paraboloid $z={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and the boundary of our infinite vertical column. One such surface is our paraboloid $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨y,\\,x,\\,2⟩{|}_{z={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨y,\\,x,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{{y}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{x}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{2}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-y\\cdot (2x)-x\\cdot (-2y)+2)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/5f/5f2f4cb03b3b0232c9debd1f4ed64bd683fe788ccba16cfbe395d9c3b629972d.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\,2\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521580 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186521614 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521580 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "18ae8c42-183b-544f-aacc-4eaa3f1d420a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521580 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186521464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521583 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "241fd184-8019-52bd-8c7e-c4c658c038e8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521583 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186521475 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521614 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186521642 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521614 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7448047a-c9c1-58cc-8181-6b27db0e6a80" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521614 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186521469 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521621 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186521580 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521621 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "840ab71a-6f7d-5c82-b51d-fda507e2a915" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521621 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186134130 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521642 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186521583 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521642 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cb01237b-4d73-5b10-94f4-c0c4f3fdada5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521642 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186134134 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543963 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543993 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186543963 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186543963 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $r(t)=\\langle - t^{2} + t, t^{3} - t^{2}, - 2 t^{2} + 2 t\\rangle$ for $t\\in [0,1).$\nGiven that $F(x,y,z)=\\langle - 2 z, 0, - 3 y\\rangle,$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\operatorname{curl}F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}S.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186543963 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "00a9d1b3-d93b-5510-80b8-e417e1c6daaf" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186543963 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=- t^{2} + t,\\quad y=t^{3} - t^{2},\\quad z=- 2 t^{2} + 2 t\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =\\langle - 2 z, 0, - 3 y\\rangle \\\\ & =\\langle - 2 \\left(- 2 t^{2} + 2 t\\right), 0, - 3 \\left(t^{3} - t^{2}\\right)\\rangle \\\\ & =\\langle 4 t^{2} - 4 t, 0, - 3 t^{3} + 3 t^{2}\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $r\\prime(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\prime(t) & =\\langle \\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(- t^{2} + t\\right), \\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(t^{3} - t^{2}\\right), \\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(- 2 t^{2} + 2 t\\right)\\rangle \\\\ & =\\langle 1 - 2 t, 3 t^{2} - 2 t, 2 - 4 t\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot r\\prime(t) & =\\langle 4 t^{2} - 4 t, 0, - 3 t^{3} + 3 t^{2}\\rangle\\cdot \\langle 1 - 2 t, 3 t^{2} - 2 t, 2 - 4 t\\rangle \\\\ & =\\left(4 t^{2} - 4 t\\right)\\cdot \\left(1 - 2 t\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\cdot \\left(3 t^{2} - 2 t\\right)+\\left(- 3 t^{3} + 3 t^{2}\\right)\\cdot \\left(2 - 4 t\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(- 8 t^{3} + 12 t^{2} - 4 t\\right)+\\left(0\\right)+\\left(12 t^{4} - 18 t^{3} + 6 t^{2}\\right) \\\\ & =12 t^{4} - 26 t^{3} + 18 t^{2} - 4 t \\\\ & =12 t^{4} - 26 t^{3} + 18 t^{2} - 4 t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\operatorname{curl}F\\cdot\\mathrm dS & =\\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm dr \\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{1} F(r(t))\\cdot r\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(12 t^{4} - 26 t^{3} + 18 t^{2} - 4 t\\right)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ & =\\left[\\left(- \\frac{13 t^{4}}{2}\\right) + \\left(- 2 t^{2}\\right) + \\left(\\frac{12 t^{5}}{5}\\right) + \\left(6 t^{3}\\right)\\right]_{0}^{1} \\\\ & =\\left(\\left(- \\frac{13}{2}\\right) + \\left(-2\\right) + \\left(\\frac{12}{5}\\right) + \\left(6\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(0\\right) + \\left(0\\right) + \\left(0\\right) + \\left(0\\right)\\right) \\\\ & =- \\frac{1}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186543965 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544000 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186543965 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186543965 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $r(t)=\\langle 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}, 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}, 1\\rangle$ for $t\\in [0,2 \\pi).$\nGiven that $F(x,y,z)=\\langle y, - x, 0\\rangle,$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\operatorname{curl}F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}S.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186543965 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "07a605d5-0ef0-5420-9db8-ccb3e82bd11b" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186543965 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\quad y=3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\quad z=1\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =\\langle y, - x, 0\\rangle \\\\ & =\\langle \\left(3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right), - \\left(2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right), 0\\rangle \\\\ & =\\langle 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}, - 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}, 0\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $r\\prime(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\prime(t) & =\\langle \\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right), \\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right), \\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(1\\right)\\rangle \\\\ & =\\langle - 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}, 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}, 0\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot r\\prime(t) & =\\langle 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}, - 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}, 0\\rangle\\cdot \\langle - 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}, 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}, 0\\rangle \\\\ & =\\left(3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\cdot \\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(- 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\cdot \\left(3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\cdot \\left(0\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(- 6 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(- 6 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(0\\right) \\\\ & =- 6 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} - 6 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\operatorname{curl}F\\cdot\\mathrm dS & =\\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm dr \\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi} F(r(t))\\cdot r\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\left(-6\\right)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ & =\\left[\\left(- 6 t\\right)\\right]_{0}^{2 \\pi} \\\\ & =\\left(\\left(- 12 \\pi\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(0\\right)\\right) \\\\ & =- 12 \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186543967 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543982 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186543967 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186543967 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $r(t)=\\langle 4 t^{3} - 4 t, 9 t^{2} + 9 t, 3 t^{3} - 3 t\\rangle$ for $t\\in [-1,0).$\nGiven that $F(x,y,z)=\\langle - z, 0, - y\\rangle,$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\operatorname{curl}F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}S.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186543967 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1c938745-fa24-53fe-8dc1-14b39296689e" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186543967 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=4 t^{3} - 4 t,\\quad y=9 t^{2} + 9 t,\\quad z=3 t^{3} - 3 t\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =\\langle - z, 0, - y\\rangle \\\\ & =\\langle - \\left(3 t^{3} - 3 t\\right), 0, - \\left(9 t^{2} + 9 t\\right)\\rangle \\\\ & =\\langle - 3 t^{3} + 3 t, 0, - 9 t^{2} - 9 t\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $r\\prime(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\prime(t) & =\\langle \\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(4 t^{3} - 4 t\\right), \\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(9 t^{2} + 9 t\\right), \\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(3 t^{3} - 3 t\\right)\\rangle \\\\ & =\\langle 12 t^{2} - 4, 18 t + 9, 9 t^{2} - 3\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot r\\prime(t) & =\\langle - 3 t^{3} + 3 t, 0, - 9 t^{2} - 9 t\\rangle\\cdot \\langle 12 t^{2} - 4, 18 t + 9, 9 t^{2} - 3\\rangle \\\\ & =\\left(- 3 t^{3} + 3 t\\right)\\cdot \\left(12 t^{2} - 4\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\cdot \\left(18 t + 9\\right)+\\left(- 9 t^{2} - 9 t\\right)\\cdot \\left(9 t^{2} - 3\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(- 36 t^{5} + 48 t^{3} - 12 t\\right)+\\left(0\\right)+\\left(- 81 t^{4} - 81 t^{3} + 27 t^{2} + 27 t\\right) \\\\ & =- 36 t^{5} - 81 t^{4} - 33 t^{3} + 27 t^{2} + 15 t \\\\ & =- 36 t^{5} - 81 t^{4} - 33 t^{3} + 27 t^{2} + 15 t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\operatorname{curl}F\\cdot\\mathrm dS & =\\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm dr \\\\ & =\\int_{-1}^{0} F(r(t))\\cdot r\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ & =\\int_{-1}^{0}\\left(- 36 t^{5} - 81 t^{4} - 33 t^{3} + 27 t^{2} + 15 t\\right)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ & =\\left[\\left(- \\frac{81 t^{5}}{5}\\right) + \\left(- 6 t^{6}\\right) + \\left(- \\frac{33 t^{4}}{4}\\right) + \\left(\\frac{15 t^{2}}{2}\\right) + \\left(9 t^{3}\\right)\\right]_{-1}^{0} \\\\ & =\\left(\\left(0\\right) + \\left(0\\right) + \\left(0\\right) + \\left(0\\right) + \\left(0\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(\\frac{81}{5}\\right) + \\left(-6\\right) + \\left(- \\frac{33}{4}\\right) + \\left(\\frac{15}{2}\\right) + \\left(-9\\right)\\right) \\\\ & =- \\frac{9}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186543978 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543977 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186543978 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186543978 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $r(t)=\\langle \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}, - 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}, 1\\rangle$ for $t\\in [0,2 \\pi).$\nGiven that $F(x,y,z)=\\langle y, - x, 0\\rangle,$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\operatorname{curl}F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}S.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186543978 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4a815ade-dfb6-5a43-b8bb-5c22f46d2670" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186543978 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\quad y=- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\quad z=1\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =\\langle y, - x, 0\\rangle \\\\ & =\\langle \\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right), - \\left(\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right), 0\\rangle \\\\ & =\\langle - 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}, - \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}, 0\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $r\\prime(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\prime(t) & =\\langle \\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right), \\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right), \\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(1\\right)\\rangle \\\\ & =\\langle - \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}, - 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}, 0\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot r\\prime(t) & =\\langle - 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}, - \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}, 0\\rangle\\cdot \\langle - \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}, - 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}, 0\\rangle \\\\ & =\\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\cdot \\left(- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(- \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\cdot \\left(- 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\cdot \\left(0\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(2 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(2 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(0\\right) \\\\ & =2 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\operatorname{curl}F\\cdot\\mathrm dS & =\\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm dr \\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi} F(r(t))\\cdot r\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\left(2\\right)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ & =\\left[\\left(2 t\\right)\\right]_{0}^{2 \\pi} \\\\ & =\\left(\\left(4 \\pi\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(0\\right)\\right) \\\\ & =4 \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186543983 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543999 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186543983 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186543983 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $r(t)=\\langle - \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}, - 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}, 1\\rangle$ for $t\\in [0,2 \\pi).$\nGiven that $F(x,y,z)=\\langle - 3 y, 3 x, 0\\rangle,$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\operatorname{curl}F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}S.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186543983 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7483bc57-3777-5a4c-b887-84c48a923ec4" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186543983 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=- \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\quad y=- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\quad z=1\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =\\langle - 3 y, 3 x, 0\\rangle \\\\ & =\\langle - 3 \\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right), 3 \\left(- \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right), 0\\rangle \\\\ & =\\langle 6 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}, - 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}, 0\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $r\\prime(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\prime(t) & =\\langle \\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(- \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right), \\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right), \\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(1\\right)\\rangle \\\\ & =\\langle \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}, - 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}, 0\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot r\\prime(t) & =\\langle 6 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}, - 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}, 0\\rangle\\cdot \\langle \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}, - 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}, 0\\rangle \\\\ & =\\left(6 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(- 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\cdot \\left(- 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\cdot \\left(0\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(6 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(6 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(0\\right) \\\\ & =6 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} + 6 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\operatorname{curl}F\\cdot\\mathrm dS & =\\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm dr \\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi} F(r(t))\\cdot r\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\left(6\\right)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ & =\\left[\\left(6 t\\right)\\right]_{0}^{2 \\pi} \\\\ & =\\left(\\left(12 \\pi\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(0\\right)\\right) \\\\ & =12 \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186543995 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543985 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186543995 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186543995 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $r(t)=\\langle 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}, - 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}, 1\\rangle$ for $t\\in [0,2 \\pi).$\nGiven that $F(x,y,z)=\\langle - 3 y, 3 x, 0\\rangle,$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\operatorname{curl}F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}S.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186543995 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "aed77857-b62d-52c0-b3e1-b8ab3820a8e9" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186543995 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\quad y=- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\quad z=1\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =\\langle - 3 y, 3 x, 