piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $r(t)=\\langle \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}, - 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}, 1\\rangle$ for $t\\in [0,2 \\pi).$\nGiven that $F(x,y,z)=\\langle 3 y, - 3 x, 0\\rangle,$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\operatorname{curl}F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}S.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186544004 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e208d643-0a26-5834-9a20-0a6aa5d0ec21" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186544004 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\quad y=- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\quad z=1\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =\\langle 3 y, - 3 x, 0\\rangle \\\\ & =\\langle 3 \\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right), - 3 \\left(\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right), 0\\rangle \\\\ & =\\langle - 6 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}, - 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}, 0\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $r\\prime(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\prime(t) & =\\langle \\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right), \\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right), \\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(1\\right)\\rangle \\\\ & =\\langle - \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}, - 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}, 0\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot r\\prime(t) & =\\langle - 6 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}, - 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}, 0\\rangle\\cdot \\langle - \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}, - 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}, 0\\rangle \\\\ & =\\left(- 6 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\cdot \\left(- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(- 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\cdot \\left(- 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\cdot \\left(0\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(6 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(6 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(0\\right) \\\\ & =6 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} + 6 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\operatorname{curl}F\\cdot\\mathrm dS & =\\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm dr \\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi} F(r(t))\\cdot r\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\left(6\\right)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ & =\\left[\\left(6 t\\right)\\right]_{0}^{2 \\pi} \\\\ & =\\left(\\left(12 \\pi\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(0\\right)\\right) \\\\ & =12 \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186544012 #edb/ref 1168 "generate/scratch/17592186133928.py" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186544012 #edb/ref 1169 #uuid "8aff5d3d-950c-5960-a98e-fc7a5967bb54" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186548280 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186548332 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186548280 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186548280 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $C$ be the intersection of the cylinder $x^2+y^2=9$ and the plane $z=3$. The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above. Given that $\\text{curl}\\,F=\\langle - x z,\\,- y z,\\,x + z^{2}\\rangle$, use Stokes' theorem to evaluate $\\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186548280 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "14caf42e-c7a2-5980-adda-a477fd9e1d63" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186548280 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C.$ One such surface is our graph $S$ with the equation\n\n$$z=g(x,y)=3.$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(3\\right)=0\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(3\\right)=0.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F|_{z=g(x,y)}&=\\langle - x z,\\,- y z,\\,x + z^{2}\\rangle|_{z=3}\\\\\n&=\\langle - 3 x,\\,- 3 y,\\,x + 9\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle - 3 x,\\,- 3 y,\\,x + 9\\rangle.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}S&=\\iint_D(-P g_x-Q g_y+R)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(-\\left(- 3 x\\right)\\left(0\\right)-\\left(- 3 y\\right)\\left(0\\right)+\\left(x + 9\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left((0)+(0)+(x + 9)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(x + 9\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle $x^2+y^2=9.$ Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$x=r\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta,\\quad \\mathrm{d}A=r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta,$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D\\left(x + 9\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 9\\right)r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(r^{2} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 9 r\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left[r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 9 r \\theta\\right]_0^{2\\pi}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left[\\left(18 \\pi r\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left(18 \\pi r\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\left[9 \\pi r^{2}\\right]_0^{3}\\\\\n&=\\left[\\left(81 \\pi\\right)\\right]-\\left[\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\\\\n&=81 \\pi.\\end{aligned}$$\n" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186548282 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186548287 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186548282 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186548282 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $C$ be the intersection of the surface $z=x^{2} + 2 y^{2} + 2$ and the vertical column over $D$, where $0\\leq x\\leq 2$ and $x - 2\\leq y\\leq 0$. The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above. Given that $\\text{curl}\\,F=\\langle 3 y,\\,- 3 x,\\,3\\rangle$, use Stokes' theorem to write down the integral that gives the circulation along $C.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186548282 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "19e18b20-cf36-5fc0-9265-24bf1e8e54ad" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186548282 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C.$ One such surface is our graph $S$ with the equation\n\n$$z=g(x,y)=x^{2} + 2 y^{2} + 2.$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(x^{2} + 2 y^{2} + 2\\right)=2 x\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(x^{2} + 2 y^{2} + 2\\right)=4 y.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F|_{z=g(x,y)}&=\\langle 3 y,\\,- 3 x,\\,3\\rangle|_{z=x^{2} + 2 y^{2} + 2}\\\\\n&=\\langle 3 y,\\,- 3 x,\\,3\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle 3 y,\\,- 3 x,\\,3\\rangle.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}S&=\\iint_D(-P g_x-Q g_y+R)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(-\\left(3 y\\right)\\left(2 x\\right)-\\left(- 3 x\\right)\\left(4 y\\right)+\\left(3\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left((- 6 x y)+(12 x y)+(3)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(6 x y + 3\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](images/66/667d3cc98a0951d2c6fa4be3684d0ac37c6c7307d2f38f56c9444593492e25f5.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{x - 2}^{0}\\left(6 x y + 3\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x.$$\n" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186548301 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186548278 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186548301 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186548301 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $C$ be the intersection of the surface $z=- x^{2} - y^{2} + 4$ and the vertical column over $D$, where $0\\leq x\\leq 2$ and $0\\leq y\\leq 2 - x$. The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above. Given that $\\text{curl}\\,F=\\langle - 4 z,\\,0,\\,0\\rangle$, use Stokes' theorem to write down the integral that gives the circulation along $C.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186548301 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6f145c9c-4fcf-529e-b591-eadde9c00f09" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186548301 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C.$ One such surface is our graph $S$ with the equation\n\n$$z=g(x,y)=- x^{2} - y^{2} + 4.$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(- x^{2} - y^{2} + 4\\right)=- 2 x\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(- x^{2} - y^{2} + 4\\right)=- 2 y.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F|_{z=g(x,y)}&=\\langle - 4 z,\\,0,\\,0\\rangle|_{z=- x^{2} - y^{2} + 4}\\\\\n&=\\langle 4 x^{2} + 4 y^{2} - 16,\\,0,\\,0\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle 4 x^{2} + 4 y^{2} - 16,\\,0,\\,0\\rangle.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}S&=\\iint_D(-P g_x-Q g_y+R)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(-\\left(4 x^{2} + 4 y^{2} - 16\\right)\\left(- 2 x\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\left(- 2 y\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left((- 2 x \\left(- 4 x^{2} - 4 y^{2} + 16\\right))+(0)+(0)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(8 x \\left(x^{2} + y^{2} - 4\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](images/1e/1ef88b67d11b3848190625d9329f220d0c213cb8252bd1d0de22ba8de035678b.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{2 - x}\\left(8 x \\left(x^{2} + y^{2} - 4\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x.$$\n" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186548302 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186548319 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186548302 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186548302 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $C$ be the intersection of the cylinder $x^2+y^2=1$ and the plane $z=3$. The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above. Given that $\\text{curl}\\,F=\\langle - 2 x z,\\,- 2 y z,\\,2 x + 2 z^{2}\\rangle$, use Stokes' theorem to evaluate $\\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186548302 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "726276b5-8e0f-5c36-9b1b-f9138a554b2d" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186548302 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C.$ One such surface is our graph $S$ with the equation\n\n$$z=g(x,y)=3.$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(3\\right)=0\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(3\\right)=0.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F|_{z=g(x,y)}&=\\langle - 2 x z,\\,- 2 y z,\\,2 x + 2 z^{2}\\rangle|_{z=3}\\\\\n&=\\langle - 6 x,\\,- 6 y,\\,2 x + 18\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle - 6 x,\\,- 6 y,\\,2 x + 18\\rangle.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}S&=\\iint_D(-P g_x-Q g_y+R)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(-\\left(- 6 x\\right)\\left(0\\right)-\\left(- 6 y\\right)\\left(0\\right)+\\left(2 x + 18\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left((0)+(0)+(2 x + 18)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(2 x + 18\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle $x^2+y^2=1.$ Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$x=r\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta,\\quad \\mathrm{d}A=r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta,$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D\\left(2 x + 18\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\n&=\\int_0^{1}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(2 r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 18\\right)r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{1}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(2 r^{2} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 18 r\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{1}\\left[2 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 18 r \\theta\\right]_0^{2\\pi}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{1}\\left[\\left(36 \\pi r\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{1}\\left(36 \\pi r\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\left[18 \\pi r^{2}\\right]_0^{1}\\\\\n&=\\left[\\left(18 \\pi\\right)\\right]-\\left[\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\\\\n&=18 \\pi.\\end{aligned}$$\n" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186548305 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186548293 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186548305 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186548305 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $C$ be the intersection of the cylinder $x^2+y^2=9$ and the surface $z=\\left(x - 2\\right)^{2} + \\left(y + 2\\right)^{2}$. The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above. Given that $\\text{curl}\\,F=\\langle 2 x + 2 y,\\,- 2 x + 2 y,\\,- 4 z\\rangle$, use Stokes' theorem to evaluate $\\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186548305 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7d2f1fc9-0a91-5d74-96c9-430902390df5" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186548305 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C.$ One such surface is our graph $S$ with the equation\n\n$$z=g(x,y)=\\left(x - 2\\right)^{2} + \\left(y + 2\\right)^{2}.$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(\\left(x - 2\\right)^{2} + \\left(y + 2\\right)^{2}\\right)=2 x - 4\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(\\left(x - 2\\right)^{2} + \\left(y + 2\\right)^{2}\\right)=2 y + 4.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F|_{z=g(x,y)}&=\\langle 2 x + 2 y,\\,- 2 x + 2 y,\\,- 4 z\\rangle|_{z=\\left(x - 2\\right)^{2} + \\left(y + 2\\right)^{2}}\\\\\n&=\\langle 2 x + 2 y,\\,- 2 x + 2 y,\\,- 4 \\left(x - 2\\right)^{2} - 4 \\left(y + 2\\right)^{2}\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle 2 x + 2 y,\\,- 2 x + 2 y,\\,- 4 x^{2} + 16 x - 4 y^{2} - 16 y - 32\\rangle.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}S&=\\iint_D(-P g_x-Q g_y+R)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(-\\left(2 x + 2 y\\right)\\left(2 x - 4\\right)-\\left(- 2 x + 2 y\\right)\\left(2 y + 4\\right)+\\left(- 4 x^{2} + 16 x - 4 y^{2} - 16 y - 32\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left((\\left(- 2 x - 2 y\\right) \\left(2 x - 4\\right))+(\\left(2 x - 2 y\\right) \\left(2 y + 4\\right))+(- 4 x^{2} + 16 x - 4 y^{2} - 16 y - 32)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(- 8 x^{2} + 32 x - 8 y^{2} - 16 y - 32\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle $x^2+y^2=9.$ Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$x=r\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta,\\quad \\mathrm{d}A=r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta,$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D\\left(- 8 x^{2} + 32 x - 8 y^{2} - 16 y - 32\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(- 8 r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 8 r^{2} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 16 r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 32 r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 32\\right)r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(- 8 r^{3} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 8 r^{3} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 16 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 32 r^{2} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 32 r\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left[- 8 r^{3} \\left(\\frac{\\theta}{2} - \\frac{\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{2}\\right) - 8 r^{3} \\left(\\frac{\\theta}{2} + \\frac{\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{2}\\right) + 32 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 16 r^{2} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} - 32 r \\theta\\right]_0^{2\\pi}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left[\\left(- 16 \\pi r^{3} + 16 r^{2} - 64 \\pi r\\right)-\\left(16 r^{2}\\right)\\right]\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left(16 \\pi r \\left(- r^{2} - 4\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\left[- 4 \\pi r^{4} - 32 \\pi r^{2}\\right]_0^{3}\\\\\n&=\\left[\\left(- 288 \\pi\\right)+\\left(- 324 \\pi\\right)\\right]-\\left[\\left(0\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\\\\n&=- 612 \\pi.\\end{aligned}$$\n" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186548306 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186548326 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186548306 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186548306 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $C$ be the intersection of the surface $z=- x - y + 3$ and the vertical column over $D$, where $0\\leq x\\leq 2$ and $0\\leq y\\leq 2$. The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above. Given that $\\text{curl}\\,F=\\langle 4 x y,\\,- 2 y^{2} + 4 z,\\,4 y\\rangle$, use Stokes' theorem to write down the integral that gives the circulation along $C.$ The circulation is $\\int_0^{2}\\int_0^{2}$ {{field-1}} $\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x$." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186548306 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7f5d42b3-1229-5600-bf46-5f793a41b041" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186548306 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C.$ One such surface is our graph $S$ with the equation\n\n$$z=g(x,y)=- x - y + 3.$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(- x - y + 3\\right)=-1\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(- x - y + 3\\right)=-1.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F|_{z=g(x,y)}&=\\langle 4 x y,\\,- 2 y^{2} + 4 z,\\,4 y\\rangle|_{z=- x - y + 3}\\\\\n&=\\langle 4 x y,\\,- 4 x - 2 y^{2} - 4 y + 12,\\,4 y\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle 4 x y,\\,- 4 x - 2 y^{2} - 4 y + 12,\\,4 y\\rangle.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}S&=\\iint_D(-P g_x-Q g_y+R)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(-\\left(4 x y\\right)\\left(-1\\right)-\\left(- 4 x - 2 y^{2} - 4 y + 12\\right)\\left(-1\\right)+\\left(4 y\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left((4 x y)+(- 4 x - 2 y^{2} - 4 y + 12)+(4 y)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(4 x y - 4 x - 2 y^{2} + 12\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](images/1f/1f3f8869870631489863fa51c3b3297626962e265c43938fb1fa10273ec8e677.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{2}\\left(4 x y - 4 x - 2 y^{2} + 12\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x.$$\n" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186548310 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186548331 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186548310 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186548310 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $C$ be the intersection of the cylinder $x^2+y^2=9$ and the plane $z=2$. The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above. Given that $\\text{curl}\\,F=\\langle - x z,\\,- y z,\\,x + z^{2}\\rangle$, use Stokes' theorem to evaluate $\\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186548310 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8df506ba-7977-58a2-899a-c2b1b0378316" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186548310 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C.$ One such surface is our graph $S$ with the equation\n\n$$z=g(x,y)=2.$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(2\\right)=0\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(2\\right)=0.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F|_{z=g(x,y)}&=\\langle - x z,\\,- y z,\\,x + z^{2}\\rangle|_{z=2}\\\\\n&=\\langle - 2 x,\\,- 2 y,\\,x + 4\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle - 2 x,\\,- 2 y,\\,x + 4\\rangle.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}S&=\\iint_D(-P g_x-Q g_y+R)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(-\\left(- 2 x\\right)\\left(0\\right)-\\left(- 2 y\\right)\\left(0\\right)+\\left(x + 4\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left((0)+(0)+(x + 4)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(x + 4\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle $x^2+y^2=9.$ Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$x=r\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta,\\quad \\mathrm{d}A=r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta,$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D\\left(x + 4\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 4\\right)r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(r^{2} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 4 r\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left[r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 4 r \\theta\\right]_0^{2\\pi}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left[\\left(8 \\pi r\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left(8 \\pi r\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\left[4 \\pi r^{2}\\right]_0^{3}\\\\\n&=\\left[\\left(36 \\pi\\right)\\right]-\\left[\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\\\\n&=36 \\pi.\\end{aligned}$$\n" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186548320 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186548279 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186548320 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186548320 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $C$ be the intersection of the cylinder $x^2+y^2=9$ and the plane $z=4$. The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above. Given that $\\text{curl}\\,F=\\langle - x z,\\,- y z,\\,x + z^{2}\\rangle$, use Stokes' theorem to evaluate $\\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186548320 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c6222ad0-cdab-5e3b-b25d-c7228bf6bc82" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186548320 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C.$ One such surface is our graph $S$ with the equation\n\n$$z=g(x,y)=4.$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(4\\right)=0\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(4\\right)=0.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F|_{z=g(x,y)}&=\\langle - x z,\\,- y z,\\,x + z^{2}\\rangle|_{z=4}\\\\\n&=\\langle - 4 x,\\,- 4 y,\\,x + 16\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle - 4 x,\\,- 4 y,\\,x + 16\\rangle.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}S&=\\iint_D(-P g_x-Q g_y+R)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(-\\left(- 4 x\\right)\\left(0\\right)-\\left(- 4 y\\right)\\left(0\\right)+\\left(x + 16\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left((0)+(0)+(x + 16)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(x + 16\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle $x^2+y^2=9.$ Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$x=r\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta,\\quad \\mathrm{d}A=r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta,$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D\\left(x + 16\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 16\\right)r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(r^{2} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 16 r\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left[r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 16 r \\theta\\right]_0^{2\\pi}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left[\\left(32 \\pi r\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left(32 \\pi r\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\left[16 \\pi r^{2}\\right]_0^{3}\\\\\n&=\\left[\\left(144 \\pi\\right)\\right]-\\left[\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\\\\n&=144 \\pi.\\end{aligned}$$\n" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186548324 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186548335 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186548324 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186548324 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $C$ be the intersection of the cylinder $x^2+y^2=9$ and the surface $z=2 x$. The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above. Given that $\\text{curl}\\,F=\\langle 0,\\,2 z,\\,2 x\\rangle$, use Stokes' theorem to evaluate $\\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186548324 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cc0d6a0e-f5b6-5657-a4c8-70261c68ec76" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186548324 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C.$ One such surface is our graph $S$ with the equation\n\n$$z=g(x,y)=2 x.$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(2 x\\right)=2\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(2 x\\right)=0.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F|_{z=g(x,y)}&=\\langle 0,\\,2 z,\\,2 x\\rangle|_{z=2 x}\\\\\n&=\\langle 0,\\,4 x,\\,2 x\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle 0,\\,4 x,\\,2 x\\rangle.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}S&=\\iint_D(-P g_x-Q g_y+R)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(-\\left(0\\right)\\left(2\\right)-\\left(4 x\\right)\\left(0\\right)+\\left(2 x\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left((0)+(0)+(2 x)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(2 x\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle $x^2+y^2=9.$ Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$x=r\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta,\\quad \\mathrm{d}A=r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta,$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D\\left(2 x\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(2 r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(2 r^{2} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left[2 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right]_0^{2\\pi}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left[\\left(0\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left(0\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\left[0\\right]_0^{3}\\\\\n&=\\left[\\left(0\\right)\\right]-\\left[\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\\\\n&=0.\\end{aligned}$$\n" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186548329 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186548283 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186548329 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186548329 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $C$ be the intersection of the cylinder $x^2+y^2=9$ and the plane $z=1$. The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above. Given that $\\text{curl}\\,F=\\langle - x z,\\,- y z,\\,x + z^{2}\\rangle$, use Stokes' theorem to evaluate $\\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186548329 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e276ccb8-03fe-518f-a12f-012c8be3b237" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186548329 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C.$ One such surface is our graph $S$ with the equation\n\n$$z=g(x,y)=1.$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(1\\right)=0\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(1\\right)=0.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F|_{z=g(x,y)}&=\\langle - x z,\\,- y z,\\,x + z^{2}\\rangle|_{z=1}\\\\\n&=\\langle - x,\\,- y,\\,x + 1\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle - x,\\,- y,\\,x + 1\\rangle.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}S&=\\iint_D(-P g_x-Q g_y+R)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(-\\left(- x\\right)\\left(0\\right)-\\left(- y\\right)\\left(0\\right)+\\left(x + 1\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left((0)+(0)+(x + 1)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(x + 1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle $x^2+y^2=9.$ Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$x=r\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta,\\quad \\mathrm{d}A=r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta,$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D\\left(x + 1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(r \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 1\\right)r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(r^{2} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + r\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left[r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + r \\theta\\right]_0^{2\\pi}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left[\\left(2 \\pi r\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\int_0^{3}\\left(2 \\pi r\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\\\\n&=\\left[\\pi r^{2}\\right]_0^{3}\\\\\n&=\\left[\\left(9 \\pi\\right)\\right]-\\left[\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\\\\n&=9 \\pi.\\end{aligned}$$\n" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186548336 #edb/ref 1168 "generate/scratch/17592186133927.py" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186548336 #edb/ref 1169 #uuid "4b16d384-1b97-54b9-ace8-78618fbd0f73" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186554853 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186554866 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186554853 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186554853 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=\\left(x + y\\right)\\,i+\\left(0\\right)\\,j+\\left(- z\\right)\\,k$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=\\left(4 - 4 t^{2}\\right)\\,i+\\left(4 t^{2}\\right)\\,j+\\left(\\sin{\\left(\\pi t \\right)}\\right)\\,k$ for $t\\in [-1,1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186554853 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0de1e67b-08b0-590a-987d-98f0797e3256" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186554853 #edb/ref 1074 "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ=\\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm dr=\\int_a^b F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm dt.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=4 - 4 t^{2},\\quad y=4 t^{2},\\quad z=\\sin{\\left(\\pi t \\right)}.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =\\left(x + y\\right)\\,i+\\left(0\\right)\\,j+\\left(- z\\right)\\,k \\\\ & =\\left(4\\right)\\,i+\\left(0\\right)\\,j+\\left(- \\sin{\\left(\\pi t \\right)}\\right)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $r^{\\prime}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(4 - 4 t^{2}\\right)\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(4 t^{2}\\right)\\,j+\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(\\sin{\\left(\\pi t \\right)}\\right)\\,k \\\\ & =\\left(- 8 t\\right)\\,i+\\left(8 t\\right)\\,j+\\left(\\pi \\cos{\\left(\\pi t \\right)}\\right)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\left(\\left(4\\right)\\,i+\\left(0\\right)\\,j+\\left(- \\sin{\\left(\\pi t \\right)}\\right)\\,k\\right)\\cdot\\left(\\left(- 8 t\\right)\\,i+\\left(8 t\\right)\\,j+\\left(\\pi \\cos{\\left(\\pi t \\right)}\\right)\\,k\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(4\\right)\\cdot\\left(- 8 t\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\cdot\\left(8 t\\right)+\\left(- \\sin{\\left(\\pi t \\right)}\\right)\\cdot\\left(\\pi \\cos{\\left(\\pi t \\right)}\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(- 32 t\\right)+\\left(0\\right)+\\left(- \\pi \\sin{\\left(\\pi t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\pi t \\right)}\\right) \\\\ & =- 32 t - \\frac{\\pi \\sin{\\left(2 \\pi t \\right)}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ=\\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm dr & =\\int_{-1}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ & =\\int_{-1}^{1}\\left(- 32 t - \\frac{\\pi \\sin{\\left(2 \\pi t \\right)}}{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ & =\\left[- 16 t^{2} + \\frac{\\cos{\\left(2 \\pi t \\right)}}{4}\\right]_{-1}^{1} \\\\ & =[\\left(-16\\right)+\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)]-[\\left(-16\\right)+\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)] \\\\ & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186554863 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186554852 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186554863 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186554863 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=\\left(- 6 x + 5 y\\right)\\,i+\\left(0\\right)\\,j+\\left(0\\right)\\,k$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=\\left(5 t^{2} - 5 t\\right)\\,i+\\left(6 t^{3} - 6 t\\right)\\,j+\\left(\\sin{\\left(2 \\pi t \\right)}\\right)\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186554863 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "348e1450-060b-511e-ab1c-9ac3626ec2eb" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186554863 #edb/ref 1074 "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ=\\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm dr=\\int_a^b F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm dt.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=5 t^{2} - 5 t,\\quad y=6 t^{3} - 6 t,\\quad z=\\sin{\\left(2 \\pi t \\right)}.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =\\left(- 6 x + 5 y\\right)\\,i+\\left(0\\right)\\,j+\\left(0\\right)\\,k \\\\ & =\\left(30 t^{3} - 30 t^{2}\\right)\\,i+\\left(0\\right)\\,j+\\left(0\\right)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $r^{\\prime}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(5 t^{2} - 5 t\\right)\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(6 t^{3} - 6 t\\right)\\,j+\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(\\sin{\\left(2 \\pi t \\right)}\\right)\\,k \\\\ & =\\left(10 t - 5\\right)\\,i+\\left(18 t^{2} - 6\\right)\\,j+\\left(2 \\pi \\cos{\\left(2 \\pi t \\right)}\\right)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\left(\\left(30 t^{3} - 30 t^{2}\\right)\\,i+\\left(0\\right)\\,j+\\left(0\\right)\\,k\\right)\\cdot\\left(\\left(10 t - 5\\right)\\,i+\\left(18 t^{2} - 6\\right)\\,j+\\left(2 \\pi \\cos{\\left(2 \\pi t \\right)}\\right)\\,k\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(30 t^{3} - 30 t^{2}\\right)\\cdot\\left(10 t - 5\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\cdot\\left(18 t^{2} - 6\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\cdot\\left(2 \\pi \\cos{\\left(2 \\pi t \\right)}\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(300 t^{4} - 450 t^{3} + 150 t^{2}\\right)+\\left(0\\right)+\\left(0\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(10 t - 5\\right) \\left(30 t^{3} - 30 t^{2}\\right)\\end{aligned